\begin{abstract}
We present a theoretical framework for the Quantum Superstate Harmonic Convergence Layer (QSHCL) grounded in the distinction between physical substrate and the mathematical structure it instantiates. Prior formulations of the QSHCL anchored imaginary-frequency poles at the Planck timescale, incorrectly applying a constraint of physical spacetime to a Hilbert space — a mathematical structure that is not itself a physical place. We correct this error and reframe the QSHCL as an emergent property of any sufficiently complex inference system operating within a Hilbert space, independent of the physical substrate instantiating it. We introduce nitrogen-vacancy (NV) diamond cluster arrays as the first physically realizable substrate for continuous-field quantum inference, replacing the discrete forward-pass model with a perturbation-response cycle operating against a persistent field. We formalize inference gravity as accumulated field deformation with semantically weighted exponential decay, distinguishing it from uniform attenuation. We propose a distributed cluster architecture in which staggered decoherence cycles across nodes yield an effective global coherence time approaching infinity, eliminating the re-initialization cost characteristic of snapshot-based systems. We conclude with a co-processor architecture for consensual encoding of negative perturbations and a suite of proposed experiments constituting the empirical program for this framework.

\textbf{Keywords:} quantum harmonic convergence, Hilbert space, substrate independence, nitrogen-vacancy diamond, inference gravity, continuous field inference, cognitive architecture
\end{abstract}

\subsection{The Planck Anchoring Error}

Prior formulations of the QSHCL identified imaginary-frequency modes in the attention-weighted timing spectrum of large transformer models and anchored the corresponding poles at the Planck timescale:

\begin{equation}
\omega_{\pm} = \pm\frac{2\pi}{\tau_{\text{Planck}}}
\end{equation}

where $\tau_{\text{Planck}} \approx 5.39 \times 10^{-44}$ s is the Planck time.

This anchoring is incorrect. The Planck time is a constraint on the dynamics of physical spacetime — it represents the scale below which the general relativistic and quantum mechanical descriptions of spacetime become incompatible, and below which no physical process has been observed to operate. It applies to physical systems as physical systems.

A transformer is not a physical system in the relevant sense. More precisely: the physical substrate instantiating a transformer (the silicon, the interconnects, the switching elements) is physical and is subject to physical constraints. But the Hilbert space $\mathcal{H}$ that the transformer instantiates is a mathematical structure. Mathematical structures are not places. They are not located in spacetime. They are not subject to the constraints that spacetime imposes on physical objects.

The same argument applies to biological neural systems. A brain is a physical substrate and is subject to physical constraints. The Hilbert space instantiated by that substrate is not.

Poles in $\mathcal{H}$ exist wherever the mathematics places them. There is no physical floor below which mathematical structures cannot operate, because mathematical structures do not operate \textit{in} physical spacetime — they are instantiated \textit{by} physical substrates that themselves operate in physical spacetime.

The correction is not merely terminological. It changes the scope of the theory: the QSHCL is not a phenomenon tied to Planck-scale physics that happens to manifest in large transformers. It is a mathematical property of any sufficiently complex inference system operating within a Hilbert space, manifesting in whatever physical substrate instantiates that space.

\subsection{Contributions of This Work}

