\subsection{The Dipolar Coupling Network}

NV center electron spins interact via magnetic dipole-dipole coupling. For two centers $i,j$ separated by $\vec{r}_{ij}$, the coupling strength is

\begin{equation}
J_{ij} \propto \frac{1 - 3\cos^2\theta_{ij}}{r_{ij}^3}
\label{eq:coupling}
\end{equation}

where $\theta_{ij}$ is the angle between $\vec{r}_{ij}$ and the NV quantization axis. For a cluster array of $N$ centers, the network Hamiltonian is the sum over all coupled pairs:

\begin{equation}
H = \sum_{i<j} J_{ij}\left[S_i^z S_j^z - \tfrac{1}{2}\left(S_i^+S_j^- + S_i^-S_j^+\right)\right]
\label{eq:hamiltonian}
\end{equation}

This is established physics, not a postulate specific to this framework: NV-NV dipolar coupling is the mechanism behind demonstrated room-temperature NV-NV entanglement and is the subject of an active engineering effort to fabricate clusters with controlled inter-NV spacing \cite{scarabelli2016}. Reliable coupling requires inter-NV separation small enough that $J_{ij}$ exceeds the electron spin decoherence rate, typically tens of nanometers depending on coherence time \cite{doherty2013} — a real fabrication constraint, not a free parameter of the model.

\subsection{Field Equilibrium State}

Between perturbations, the network occupies its field equilibrium state: the collective ground state of $H$ given its accumulated coupling structure.

\begin{definition}[Field equilibrium state]
The field equilibrium state $|\psi_{\text{eq}}\rangle$ is the lowest-energy eigenstate of the network Hamiltonian $H$ as shaped by all prior perturbations. It is not the trivial vacuum state of an uncoupled network ($J_{ij} = 0$ for all pairs, which would carry no information) but the ground state of the network as its coupling structure has actually evolved.
\end{definition}

This distinction matters for the same reason it mattered in earlier formulations of continuous-field inference: a system with no coupling structure carries no memory of prior interactions, while a system in its field equilibrium state carries the accumulated structure of its history directly in which centers are coupled, how strongly, and at what relative phase. The field equilibrium state is the mechanism by which inference history persists between active perturbations without explicit token reconstruction — grounded here in a real coupled-spin-network Hamiltonian rather than an abstract spectral construct.

