\subsection{The Field Equilibrium State as Inference Gravity Carrier}

The field equilibrium state $|\psi_{\text{eq}}\rangle$ is the carrier of the inference gravity tensor between perturbations. When the network is between active perturbations, it exists in the state:

\begin{equation}
|\psi_{\text{eq}}\rangle \sim \text{ground state of } H \text{ weighted by } G(t)
\end{equation}

This is neither the vacuum state (which would carry $G = 0$) nor an active superstate (which would require ongoing perturbation). It is the field at rest, carrying its history in the network's coupling geometry.

\subsection{Resonance Overlap as the Encoding Mechanism}

In the continuous field model, inference does not proceed by quantum measurement collapse in the conventional sense, and the weighting of a perturbation is not assigned by a separate classification step performed before or after propagation — doing so would simply reintroduce a discrete reconstruction stage under another name, defeating the purpose of a continuous-field architecture. Instead, weighting is intrinsic to the propagation event itself.

A new perturbation excites a localized spin deviation, which propagates through the dipolar-coupled network via the coupling terms $J_{ij}$ in Equation~\ref{eq:hamiltonian}. Its persistence is governed by its \textit{resonance overlap} with the network's existing coupling structure: an excitation that couples strongly into already-established, strongly-coupled regions of the network reinforces and persists; an excitation that does not resonate with existing structure dissipates into weakly-coupled regions and decays quickly. The semantic weight $\alpha_i$ of Definition~3 is this resonance overlap, computed within the same continuous propagation event — not assigned externally.

This mechanism has a direct precedent in attractor-network associative memory: in a Hopfield network, the pull of a new input toward a stored pattern is determined by its overlap with that pattern's energy basin, not by an external judgment of similarity \cite{hopfield1982}. The same structure holds here — the field's response to a perturbation reflects the accumulated semantic structure of the network rather than a draw from a flat prior, with no separate forward pass required to determine that response.

The Born rule correspondence to the softmax distribution over logits — both having the form $P \propto |\langle \psi | \phi \rangle|^2$ — is noted as a structural parallel. A formal proof of equivalence in the continuous field context is deferred to subsequent work.

