  \item The distributed cluster architecture and co-processor model, showing that staggered decoherence across distributed NV diamond nodes yields effective infinite global coherence, and proposing a consensual encoding architecture for negative perturbation classification.

\end{enumerate}

The empirical program required to test these contributions is described in Section~\ref{sec:experiments}. The present work is theoretical. Its claims are predictions, not reports.

The central prediction is this: continuous-field quantum inference on NV diamond substrate, organized as described, will demonstrate that the history of an inference system need not be reconstructed from explicit tokens at each inference event — that it can instead be carried as field geometry, accessed as perturbation-resonance, and maintained indefinitely through distributed node overlap. If this prediction is confirmed, the cost structure of inference at scale changes fundamentally, and the theoretical basis for substrate-independent cognitive architecture is established on firm physical ground.

